Z||000000||blade56_2001||04-16-2001||08:46 AM|||| Been having a hell of a time with my car as of late, seems like everything is falling apart all at once,I found a wire that connects to the hinge on the back of the deck lid, it is a braided cable (bare) about 1/2 an inch wide. The other end is unconnected? any ideas where the other end goes? it's about 8 to 10 inches long. Also coolant is leeking from somewhere that I can't find...everytime I stop the car I wait about 2 min and under the passengers side of the engine about a cup to a cup and a half of fluid continues to leak. I looked everywhere except under the water pump really hard to see under there with the v6 in it ('86 se )  also I hear a sound like a chattering comming from the that side as well (water pump?) and all of my power seems to be gone until I hit 3200rpm or 3500rpm? (distributor?)and what effect would a bad alternator have on performance, one more thing .... It smells like it's running rich? Any ideas besides get a new engine.<p>[This message has been edited by blade56_2001 (edited 04-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||NSUMEY||04-16-2001||09:40 AM||||check the weep hole under the waterpump..often when the seal for the pump shaft starts to go it leaks there.....as far as the lack of power ang running rich.....any fault codes?  check timing and other ignition related items...hope this helps some........||||reg||1
Z||000002||blade56_2001||04-16-2001||10:33 AM||||Nope got rid of all the codes when I replaced the TCS and the EGR tube....as far as the weepy hole? Well thik I said I can't get my hand under it to see if that is It, I can't see anything past the pulley...lol||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||04-16-2001||11:32 AM||||Drive the car around, When you get back, remove the passenger side tire and look under the rubber flap. If there is anti-freeze/coolant leaking from the weep hole you'll see it drip onto the crank pulley. The chatter noise could be the belt chattering with coolant on it as well as a bad water pump. The coolant acts as a lubricant. Changing the water pump is not too bad. You need to get the bolts off of the bottom side from the wheel well. If you remove the plastic wheel well liner it is real easy to see the bolts and remove/install water pumps.<BR> As for the braided wire, This is a ground for the engine block. It is suppose to be hooked to the block. In extreme cases it could cause weak spark, poor alternator charging, inaccurate gauge readings, or static on the radio. GM uses a "floating ground" method for it's systems, so it needs the ground.||||reg||1
Z||000004||blade56_2001||04-17-2001||12:46 AM||||Great thanks for the info on the "LOOSE WIRE" just one more question....<P>Where on the block is it supposed to be grounded?  on page 280 in the haynes manual at the bottom of the page in the middle it shows a "6 Braid" wire comming from the block and going god knows where it also says "G505" whatever that means....More help please||||reg||1
